## What it saves and who can see it

This is the part most worth reading before you switch anything on. Each meeting gets its own page in claude.ai. Anthropic says that page holds the notes, the transcript and anything Claude built, and that people from your organisation who were on the invite can see and edit what Claude made. The page can be shared with others in the organisation. Later, users can ask Claude about past meetings they attended.

According to the announcement, Claude saves a transcript, a summary and descriptions of what was on screen. It says it does not save audio. When Claude joins, it appears as a participant and posts a notice in the call chat. Anthropic advises checking that everyone is comfortable being transcribed before you add Claude or turn on auto-join, and that is sensible advice whichever tool you use. Whether a recording or transcript is legal in your location is a separate question, and this post is general information, not legal advice.

## Outside guests and connected tools

By default, Claude stays out of calls when someone from outside the organisation is invited, and it leaves if an outside person joins part-way through. An admin can allow outside guests. In that case Claude posts its notice in the call chat but does not reply there, and what guests say still goes into the notes and transcript. If you often meet clients or suppliers, that default is worth knowing about.

Connected tools follow a permission rule. Each person's tools are used with that person's permission: Claude asks the first time, and a yes applies from then on. Answers posted in the call chat are visible to everyone on the call. For voice requests, Anthropic says that for now Claude asks each time to confirm who is speaking.

## What it costs during the beta

Anthropic says notes and transcripts are free on Team and Enterprise plans during the beta. Answers in calls are also free, unless an answer uses one of your connected tools, in which case it counts toward your plan's usage. Docs, whiteboards and other artifacts made in a call are free through 10 February 2027. The page gives no price beyond those terms, so what happens after the free periods is not stated.

## What is not available yet

The announcement lists a few things as still to come. Other video platforms are described as coming soon. Anthropic says it is working with Gong, Attention, Granola, Fireflies.ai, Otter and Plaud so that users can soon send a transcript to their tools. Outside-guest participation is off until an admin allows it. We have not seen the separate Help Center setup article, so step-by-step instructions are not covered here.

## What to check before you try it

- **Your plan.** The pages mention Team and Enterprise only.
- **Your platform.** Google Meet is the only supported platform named.
- **Your admin settings.** On Team plans, inviting Claude is on by default, so an admin who does not want it needs to switch it off.
- **Your people.** Tell attendees that Claude is joining and that a transcript will be kept.
- **Your guests.** Know that Claude stays out of calls with outsiders unless an admin changes that.

## The takeaway

Claude Meetings is a beta that the pages place on Team and Enterprise plans, and Anthropic describes a visible participant rather than a silent recorder. The details that matter most are who controls it (admins), what is kept (transcript, summary, screen descriptions, no audio) and who is left out for now (other plans, other platforms, certain compliance setups). The two pages give no price beyond the beta terms, and we did not test the product.
